WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.770
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.619
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.360
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.769
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:18.840
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.640
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.690
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is part

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.910
three, the final part, of our mini-series

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.490
on blasphemy and free expression.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.339
In part one, we traced the history of

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.620
blasphemy in the English-speaking world,

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.600
from a student hanged in Edinburgh

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.100
to the abolition of blasphemy law in

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.130
2008 to modern calls to bring it back.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.389
In part two, we told the first part

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.700
of the story of Salman Rushdie and

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.940
The Satanic Verses — the book, the

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.399
protests, who Khomeini was, and

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.457
the fatwa of Valentine's Day 1989.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.310
Today, we’ll pick up the story where we

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.060
left off, so if you haven’t listened to

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.490
the previous episode, now is the time

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.730
to press pause and listen to that one.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.969
OK, with that disclaimer out of

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:11.520
the way, let’s get right into it.

00:01:13.309 --> 00:01:16.020
I said we’d pick up the story directly

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.140
where we left off, on the 14th of

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.300
February, 1989, after the Supreme

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.530
Leader of Iran, Ayatollah Khomeini,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.450
issued his fatwa against Salman Rushdie.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:30.130
If you’ll allow me, first I’d like

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.630
to transport you a few decades

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.018
later, to the 12th of August, 2022.

00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:40.374
The setting for what I’m about to

00:01:40.374 --> 00:01:44.214
describe is not a hidden safe house in

00:01:44.214 --> 00:01:47.094
the English countryside or some secret

00:01:47.114 --> 00:01:50.984
apartment in Mumbai; it is the Chautauqua

00:01:51.054 --> 00:01:53.314
Institution, a nineteenth-century

00:01:53.394 --> 00:01:56.024
adult education retreat on the shores

00:01:56.044 --> 00:01:58.904
of a lake in western New York state.

00:01:59.904 --> 00:02:02.294
Every summer, thousands of people come

00:02:02.294 --> 00:02:04.984
here for lectures and discussions.

00:02:05.544 --> 00:02:09.214
It's quiet, thoughtful, and civilised.

00:02:09.614 --> 00:02:12.214
It’s exactly the kind of place you might

00:02:12.214 --> 00:02:15.574
go to hear a famous novelist give a talk.

00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:19.084
Salman Rushdie is on the stage.

00:02:20.144 --> 00:02:21.994
He is seventy-five years old.

00:02:22.804 --> 00:02:25.764
He is, by this point, a celebrated

00:02:25.764 --> 00:02:28.504
figure in New York literary life.

00:02:28.994 --> 00:02:32.494
He is visible, accessible, a regular

00:02:32.654 --> 00:02:34.774
at events and dinner parties.

00:02:35.674 --> 00:02:39.384
He still has a security detail, but the

00:02:39.384 --> 00:02:42.314
years of hiding are long behind him.

00:02:43.504 --> 00:02:44.914
He has written many books

00:02:45.274 --> 00:02:46.694
since The Satanic Verses.

00:02:47.124 --> 00:02:49.144
He has been knighted by the Queen,

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:52.244
so he is Sir Salman Rushdie,

00:02:52.424 --> 00:02:53.894
to give him his proper title.

00:02:54.894 --> 00:02:58.654
He is, as far as anyone can tell, safe.

00:02:59.924 --> 00:03:02.064
It’s early morning, and Rushdie

00:03:02.104 --> 00:03:03.674
is about to speak to the

00:03:03.674 --> 00:03:05.964
crowd of literary enthusiasts.

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:08.954
Suddenly, a young man with a black

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:12.224
mask jumps onto the stage and rushes

00:03:12.494 --> 00:03:14.374
towards the celebrated author.

00:03:15.404 --> 00:03:18.464
He pulls out a knife and stabs

00:03:18.544 --> 00:03:21.164
Rushdie repeatedly, fifteen

00:03:21.164 --> 00:03:23.514
times, before anyone can stop him.

00:03:24.624 --> 00:03:27.944
Rushdie is airlifted to hospital, with the

00:03:27.944 --> 00:03:31.014
doctors reporting that it’s touch and go.

00:03:31.974 --> 00:03:34.374
The injuries nearly kill him.

00:03:35.054 --> 00:03:37.464
He loses the sight in one eye, and

00:03:37.464 --> 00:03:40.504
the use of one hand, but he survives.

00:03:41.984 --> 00:03:44.724
The attacker is apprehended at the scene.

00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:47.134
He is a twenty-four-year-old

00:03:47.344 --> 00:03:49.674
Lebanese-American from New Jersey.

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:53.024
The police don’t have to scratch

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:55.534
their heads too much as to a motive,

00:03:56.074 --> 00:03:58.624
but it turns out that the would-be

00:03:58.624 --> 00:04:00.634
killer had only read a couple

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:02.644
of pages of The Satanic Verses.

00:04:03.844 --> 00:04:06.794
He was not motivated, he said, by the

00:04:06.804 --> 00:04:09.404
offensive content of the novel, but rather

00:04:09.564 --> 00:04:12.294
his admiration for Ayatollah Khomeini.

00:04:13.554 --> 00:04:17.904
Now, Rushdie almost died, but he was not

00:04:17.914 --> 00:04:20.594
the only victim of the Ayatollah’s fatwa.

00:04:21.614 --> 00:04:24.144
And indeed, to clarify, the fatwa

00:04:24.314 --> 00:04:26.914
was not limited to Rushdie alone, but

00:04:26.914 --> 00:04:29.544
rather anyone connected with the book.

00:04:29.964 --> 00:04:32.337
Everyone who had helped to spread

00:04:32.337 --> 00:04:34.694
it, through translating, publishing,

00:04:34.924 --> 00:04:37.234
and selling this blasphemous work.

00:04:38.514 --> 00:04:40.374
The first attack had come

00:04:40.474 --> 00:04:42.387
two years after the fatwa.

00:04:43.694 --> 00:04:46.324
Hitoshi Igarashi was a professor

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:48.324
at Tsukuba University in Japan.

00:04:48.884 --> 00:04:50.934
He was the Japanese translator

00:04:51.184 --> 00:04:52.454
of The Satanic Verses.

00:04:53.474 --> 00:04:57.544
On the 11th of July, 1991, he was stabbed

00:04:57.594 --> 00:05:00.314
to death in his office at the university.

00:05:01.154 --> 00:05:02.664
He was forty-four years old.

00:05:03.794 --> 00:05:06.034
His killer was never identified.

00:05:06.384 --> 00:05:09.694
And the case remains officially unsolved.

00:05:10.862 --> 00:05:13.952
The same month, in Milan, the Italian

00:05:13.952 --> 00:05:15.902
translator of the novel, Ettore

00:05:15.902 --> 00:05:18.792
Capriolo, was attacked in his apartment.

00:05:19.322 --> 00:05:20.632
He was stabbed several

00:05:20.632 --> 00:05:22.522
times and left for dead.

00:05:23.692 --> 00:05:25.602
Fortunately, he survived.

00:05:26.712 --> 00:05:30.692
In October 1993, William Nygaard — the

00:05:30.812 --> 00:05:33.052
Norwegian publisher of the book — was

00:05:33.052 --> 00:05:36.722
shot three times outside his home in Oslo.

00:05:37.682 --> 00:05:39.622
He survived, but barely.

00:05:41.002 --> 00:05:42.772
And in the weeks after the

00:05:42.802 --> 00:05:44.930
fatwa,  bookshops in Britain

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.660
received bomb threats.

00:05:46.983 --> 00:05:47.943
Two were bombed.

00:05:48.543 --> 00:05:50.513
Several American chains of bookshops

00:05:50.743 --> 00:05:53.023
removed the novel from their shelves.

00:05:54.343 --> 00:05:57.443
This was a powerful message; touch

00:05:57.443 --> 00:06:00.333
this book, do anything that helps

00:06:00.353 --> 00:06:03.063
spread its message, and you will

00:06:03.063 --> 00:06:04.703
be putting your life in danger.

00:06:06.283 --> 00:06:08.043
The effect on the publishing

00:06:08.043 --> 00:06:10.283
world was real and lasting.

00:06:11.283 --> 00:06:12.643
After the Rushdie affair,

00:06:13.073 --> 00:06:15.263
publishers around the world became

00:06:15.263 --> 00:06:17.563
more cautious about books that

00:06:17.563 --> 00:06:19.503
might attract similar responses.

00:06:20.403 --> 00:06:22.963
Some editors declined projects

00:06:23.133 --> 00:06:24.653
they might otherwise have taken on.

00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:27.713
The calculation behind a publishing

00:06:27.713 --> 00:06:30.083
decision began to include a question

00:06:30.483 --> 00:06:32.093
it had never previously included:

00:06:32.913 --> 00:06:34.863
could someone get killed for this?

00:06:36.003 --> 00:06:38.273
This is what Rushdie has called the

00:06:38.343 --> 00:06:41.263
true cost of the fatwa — not just what

00:06:41.263 --> 00:06:44.433
happened to him, but the invisible books

00:06:44.633 --> 00:06:46.753
that were never written, never published,

00:06:47.063 --> 00:06:49.483
never translated, because someone

00:06:49.793 --> 00:06:52.983
somewhere decided the risk was too high.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:55.873
Now, while all of this was

00:06:55.873 --> 00:06:58.363
happening, Rushdie had disappeared.

00:06:59.273 --> 00:07:00.423
Not entirely.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:03.583
But the life he had been living — the

00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:06.633
dinners, the literary events, the freedom

00:07:06.633 --> 00:07:09.363
to walk down a street on a whim — it

00:07:09.403 --> 00:07:11.883
all ended the day the news came through.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.900
He was given a cover name: Joseph

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.420
Anton, which he took from the first

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.300
names of two writers he admired,

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.010
Joseph Conrad and Anton Chekhov.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.990
He used that name for nearly a decade.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.700
He has written about his daily reality

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.530
in a memoir titled Joseph Anton.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.420
He chose, interestingly, to write

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.520
about himself in the third person — as

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.510
"he" rather than "I" — as though

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.820
describing someone else entirely.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.813
What he describes is an existence of

00:07:48.883 --> 00:07:51.843
constant movement and constant precaution.

00:07:52.603 --> 00:07:53.423
Safe houses.

00:07:53.803 --> 00:07:54.763
Police escorts.

00:07:55.083 --> 00:07:57.483
The total loss of spontaneity.

00:07:58.213 --> 00:08:00.823
Every journey, every appearance,

00:08:00.993 --> 00:08:02.963
every dinner at a friend's house

00:08:03.243 --> 00:08:05.043
requiring advance planning and

00:08:05.043 --> 00:08:07.193
negotiation with his protection team.

00:08:08.223 --> 00:08:10.363
He writes about the small things.

00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:13.413
The first time he was able to browse

00:08:13.433 --> 00:08:16.703
in a bookshop — just standing in front

00:08:16.703 --> 00:08:18.703
of a shelf, looking at the spines

00:08:19.073 --> 00:08:21.323
— without a bodyguard at his shoulder.

00:08:22.003 --> 00:08:23.773
It took years before

00:08:23.773 --> 00:08:24.973
he was able to do this.

00:08:26.173 --> 00:08:27.543
He has described the whole

00:08:27.543 --> 00:08:31.143
experience as a kind of living death.

00:08:31.663 --> 00:08:33.253
He was alive.

00:08:33.793 --> 00:08:35.383
But he was not free.

00:08:36.857 --> 00:08:39.387
And as you might imagine, while

00:08:39.397 --> 00:08:41.577
Rushdie was forced into hiding,

00:08:41.977 --> 00:08:44.197
the literary world was arguing.

00:08:45.207 --> 00:08:47.277
Some rallied to Rushdie's

00:08:47.297 --> 00:08:49.620
defence with complete conviction.

00:08:50.281 --> 00:08:53.193
Others said he had brought it on himself.

00:08:54.243 --> 00:08:56.483
This debate ran on for months.

00:08:56.573 --> 00:08:59.143
It was fierce, and played out in

00:08:59.153 --> 00:09:01.723
newspaper columns and television panels.

00:09:03.133 --> 00:09:06.520
One of the most prominent voices was John

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.893
le Carré, the celebrated author of spy

00:09:09.953 --> 00:09:12.883
novels like Tinker, Tailor, Soldier, Spy.

00:09:14.173 --> 00:09:17.413
He wrote a piece arguing that freedom

00:09:17.433 --> 00:09:20.993
of expression was not unlimited, that a

00:09:20.993 --> 00:09:24.213
writer had a responsibility to consider

00:09:24.243 --> 00:09:26.273
the consequences of their words,

00:09:26.613 --> 00:09:29.483
and that Rushdie had been reckless.

00:09:30.751 --> 00:09:33.673
Rushdie’s reply was simple: the

00:09:33.673 --> 00:09:36.123
moment you accept that a writer

00:09:36.123 --> 00:09:38.233
must soften their work because a

00:09:38.243 --> 00:09:40.973
group of people may react violently,

00:09:41.443 --> 00:09:44.503
you have handed that group a veto.

00:09:45.603 --> 00:09:47.693
Not through the law, but

00:09:47.723 --> 00:09:49.983
through the threat of violence.

00:09:51.113 --> 00:09:53.353
And if you grant that veto,

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:55.163
you have not protected anyone.

00:09:55.703 --> 00:09:57.533
You have simply made violence an

00:09:57.533 --> 00:09:59.943
effective tool for silencing people.

00:10:01.063 --> 00:10:04.283
The exchange ran for weeks, in letters

00:10:04.293 --> 00:10:06.373
and articles in the British press.

00:10:07.383 --> 00:10:09.573
Two of the most celebrated novelists

00:10:09.573 --> 00:10:12.553
in Britain, arguing in public about the

00:10:12.553 --> 00:10:14.943
most fundamental questions in literature.

00:10:16.453 --> 00:10:18.123
The children’s author Roald

00:10:18.133 --> 00:10:20.183
Dahl went even further.

00:10:21.193 --> 00:10:23.733
He published a piece calling Rushdie

00:10:23.973 --> 00:10:26.533
a "dangerous opportunist" who had

00:10:26.543 --> 00:10:28.773
deliberately set out to provoke

00:10:28.813 --> 00:10:31.653
Muslims and had shown no care for

00:10:31.653 --> 00:10:33.203
what he was unleashing on others.

00:10:33.941 --> 00:10:36.160
On the other side, the famous

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.280
essayist and journalist, Christopher

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:40.210
Hitchens, became one of the

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.310
most vocal defenders of Rushdie.

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:45.880
Hitchens went further than most.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.030
He argued that the fatwa was not

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:51.380
primarily a religious matter at all.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.370
It was a political act — a deliberate

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.800
attempt by the Iranian state to intimidate

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.320
writers, publishers, and governments

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.590
around the world into accepting that

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.920
certain subjects were off-limits.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.200
The correct response, he argued,

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.730
was not sympathy or concessions.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.170
It was to defend free expression

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:14.890
without qualification.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.720
As the old saying goes, "I disapprove

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:21.540
of what you say, but I will defend

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.576
to the death your right to say it".

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:27.020
But the debate was not just

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.490
between famous names in newspapers.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.520
After the fatwa, journalists took to the

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.130
streets of Britain, travelling to Bradford

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:39.150
and Birmingham to ask local British

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.570
Muslims what they thought of the fatwa.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.810
What many viewers found unsettling

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.250
was not just what some people

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:51.060
said, but who was saying it.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:54.950
These were not distant figures from

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.900
some foreign world, elderly men with

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.340
thick accents in traditional dress.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:03.660
Many were young men who had grown up

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.710
in Britain, gone to school in Britain,

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.640
and spoke with local English accents.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.170
They wore jeans and played football.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.960
And some of them said, quite calmly,

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:17.470
that Rushdie deserved to die.

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.320
Some added that they wouldn’t

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.610
necessarily do it themselves, while

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.900
others proudly declared that if they

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:26.970
had the chance, they would kill him.

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:30.420
For a certain kind of liberal Britain,

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.570
these interviews were deeply troubling.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:36.960
They suggested that integration,

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:40.030
and the idea that second-generation

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.430
immigrants would naturally absorb

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.640
British values about free speech and

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.530
tolerance, well, it was not happening

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.540
in the way people had assumed.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:53.140
Or that it was happening alongside

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.450
a religious identity that could,

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.200
on certain questions, override it.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:01.520
Now, whether that reading was

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.130
fair or not is a different debate.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.610
But the images stuck.

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.740
Capital punishment had been effectively

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.800
abolished two decades earlier, and the

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.200
last case of someone being executed for

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.010
blasphemy was three centuries earlier.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:21.940
Yet the BBC was showing footage of

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.630
young British men nodding their heads

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.430
to the command by a head of a foreign

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.240
state that a prominent British citizen

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:32.570
should be killed for his words.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.440
Now, to clarify, of course there

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.430
was not one uniform opinion in

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:40.960
the British Asian community.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.000
Many disagreed with it, and the TV

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.940
programmes that showed interviews

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.740
with people supporting the fatwa

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.870
also included interviews with

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:51.682
people who disagreed with it.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:54.470
But in the public imagination,

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.630
they were often lumped together

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:58.670
into one frightening picture.

00:13:59.856 --> 00:14:02.836
And in a perhaps even more surreal

00:14:02.906 --> 00:14:05.176
turn of events, the singer Cat

00:14:05.176 --> 00:14:07.696
Stevens got caught up in this affair.

00:14:08.646 --> 00:14:10.956
He was one of the most beloved British

00:14:10.996 --> 00:14:13.936
singer-songwriters of the 1970s: classics

00:14:13.936 --> 00:14:16.456
like Wild World and Father and Son.

00:14:17.836 --> 00:14:21.016
He had converted to Islam in 1977,

00:14:21.506 --> 00:14:23.896
changed his name to Yusuf Islam,

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:25.736
and stepped away from music.

00:14:26.426 --> 00:14:28.786
He was probably the most famous

00:14:28.796 --> 00:14:30.756
British convert to Islam.

00:14:31.636 --> 00:14:34.936
And so, in 1989, shortly after

00:14:34.936 --> 00:14:36.836
the fatwa was issued, he was

00:14:36.866 --> 00:14:38.466
asked what he thought of it.

00:14:39.566 --> 00:14:42.036
And he responded that it was

00:14:42.096 --> 00:14:45.816
clear: Rushdie deserved to die for

00:14:45.846 --> 00:14:47.356
insulting the prophet Muhammad.

00:14:48.486 --> 00:14:50.486
It was all over the news, with

00:14:50.496 --> 00:14:53.216
headlines like “Cat says kill Rushdie”,

00:14:53.676 --> 00:14:55.996
and it has dogged him ever since.

00:14:57.416 --> 00:14:59.636
In later interviews, Stevens

00:14:59.896 --> 00:15:02.036
distanced himself from these remarks,

00:15:02.106 --> 00:15:04.076
and said they had been taken out

00:15:04.076 --> 00:15:06.096
of context and he didn’t really

00:15:06.096 --> 00:15:07.756
understand what he was talking about.

00:15:08.846 --> 00:15:11.996
Still, for many people watching, it was

00:15:11.996 --> 00:15:15.736
a disorientating moment; this British

00:15:15.756 --> 00:15:18.586
singer siding with an Iranian cleric

00:15:19.036 --> 00:15:21.096
rather than the laws of his own country.

00:15:22.556 --> 00:15:25.106
Now, while all of this was being argued

00:15:25.106 --> 00:15:27.596
in newspapers and television studios,

00:15:27.836 --> 00:15:30.356
the diplomatic and political fallout

00:15:30.596 --> 00:15:32.436
was playing out at a different level.

00:15:33.516 --> 00:15:35.976
The reaction in the Western world was

00:15:36.016 --> 00:15:38.906
almost uniformly one of condemnation.

00:15:39.726 --> 00:15:41.466
The British government recalled

00:15:41.476 --> 00:15:43.246
its ambassador from Tehran.

00:15:43.616 --> 00:15:46.466
Iran recalled its ambassador from London.

00:15:47.046 --> 00:15:49.376
Diplomatic relations were suspended.

00:15:50.296 --> 00:15:51.586
The European Community

00:15:51.836 --> 00:15:53.776
issued a joint condemnation.

00:15:54.346 --> 00:15:56.216
Writers and intellectuals around the

00:15:56.216 --> 00:15:58.726
world rallied to Rushdie's defence.

00:15:59.394 --> 00:16:02.676
PEN International, the organisation that

00:16:02.676 --> 00:16:04.576
campaigns for freedom of expression,

00:16:04.966 --> 00:16:07.176
made his case a defining cause.

00:16:08.045 --> 00:16:11.081
No religious authority, in any country,

00:16:11.501 --> 00:16:13.891
had the right to sentence a writer

00:16:13.921 --> 00:16:16.141
to death for a work of fiction.

00:16:17.531 --> 00:16:19.861
Rushdie responded to the pressure

00:16:20.171 --> 00:16:22.701
by issuing a statement expressing

00:16:22.711 --> 00:16:25.031
regret for the distress caused

00:16:25.181 --> 00:16:26.991
to sincere Muslim believers.

00:16:28.051 --> 00:16:31.601
It was a half-apology, and importantly,

00:16:31.851 --> 00:16:33.841
he did not retract anything.

00:16:35.051 --> 00:16:37.951
And Khomeini too stuck to his guns.

00:16:38.471 --> 00:16:42.081
He issued a follow-up clarification: even

00:16:42.111 --> 00:16:44.951
if Rushdie repented and became the most

00:16:44.961 --> 00:16:48.501
devout man alive, it would remain the duty

00:16:48.621 --> 00:16:51.261
of every Muslim to carry out the sentence.

00:16:51.921 --> 00:16:54.131
There was no way back.

00:16:55.601 --> 00:16:59.161
Now, Khomeini died in June 1989,

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:01.361
four months after the fatwa.

00:17:02.651 --> 00:17:05.521
After his death, he ascended to an

00:17:05.521 --> 00:17:08.251
almost prophetic status; nothing

00:17:08.431 --> 00:17:09.981
he said could be questioned.

00:17:11.101 --> 00:17:12.971
His successor, Ayatollah

00:17:12.981 --> 00:17:14.761
Khamenei, reaffirmed it.

00:17:15.531 --> 00:17:17.641
But the practical situation

00:17:17.981 --> 00:17:20.811
began, slowly, to change.

00:17:22.001 --> 00:17:25.471
In 1998, the Iranian government under

00:17:25.471 --> 00:17:28.071
the reformist President Mohammad Khatami

00:17:28.371 --> 00:17:31.851
made a public statement: Iran would not

00:17:31.861 --> 00:17:33.511
support the carrying out of the fatwa

00:17:33.911 --> 00:17:36.441
and considered the matter resolved.

00:17:37.721 --> 00:17:39.731
This, however, was not

00:17:39.741 --> 00:17:41.198
the same as lifting it.

00:17:41.701 --> 00:17:44.001
But it changed the calculation.

00:17:45.163 --> 00:17:47.911
Rushdie's protection was scaled back.

00:17:48.331 --> 00:17:50.001
He began to travel more freely.

00:17:50.461 --> 00:17:52.101
He moved to New York.

00:17:52.461 --> 00:17:54.161
He resumed a public life.

00:17:55.031 --> 00:17:57.641
Over time, he seemed, by

00:17:57.641 --> 00:17:59.431
all appearances, safe.

00:18:00.791 --> 00:18:02.941
Which brings us back to Chautauqua.

00:18:03.586 --> 00:18:06.711
To Hadi Matar, who had never been to

00:18:06.711 --> 00:18:09.591
Iran, who had not been recruited by any

00:18:09.601 --> 00:18:12.601
organisation, and who had only read a

00:18:12.661 --> 00:18:15.131
couple of pages of The Satanic Verses.

00:18:16.151 --> 00:18:17.061
He had read enough.

00:18:17.551 --> 00:18:19.281
He had watched the videos of Khomeini

00:18:19.301 --> 00:18:22.411
online and decided that he would

00:18:22.421 --> 00:18:25.401
do as the Ayatollah had commanded.

00:18:26.861 --> 00:18:29.271
As you heard, Rushdie survived.

00:18:29.691 --> 00:18:32.301
He lost an eye and the movement in one

00:18:32.301 --> 00:18:35.321
hand, but he has continued to write.

00:18:36.051 --> 00:18:37.591
A memoir of the attack

00:18:37.591 --> 00:18:40.071
itself, suitably titled Knife.

00:18:40.811 --> 00:18:43.301
And now he is back to writing fiction.

00:18:44.251 --> 00:18:46.481
He published a collection of short stories

00:18:46.721 --> 00:18:50.411
last year, in 2025, and says he plans to

00:18:50.411 --> 00:18:52.911
keep on writing until he no longer can.

00:18:54.091 --> 00:18:57.511
Now, perhaps the Rushdie affair can feel

00:18:57.521 --> 00:19:00.581
like a story from another era, a religious

00:19:00.581 --> 00:19:03.351
declaration being issued by an old man

00:19:03.621 --> 00:19:05.901
born at the turn of the 20th century.

00:19:07.171 --> 00:19:09.691
In reality, the questions the affair

00:19:09.691 --> 00:19:12.791
raised are still far from resolved.

00:19:13.831 --> 00:19:16.601
The first question is the one le Carré and

00:19:16.601 --> 00:19:19.561
Rushdie were arguing about: does freedom

00:19:19.591 --> 00:19:22.331
of expression come with responsibilities?

00:19:22.891 --> 00:19:25.091
Can a writer write whatever they

00:19:25.091 --> 00:19:27.631
want, or should they take into

00:19:27.631 --> 00:19:29.571
account the perspective of people

00:19:29.861 --> 00:19:31.741
who might be offended by their words?

00:19:32.891 --> 00:19:35.051
The second is about what freedom

00:19:35.061 --> 00:19:37.071
of expression actually means.

00:19:38.261 --> 00:19:40.911
Freedom of expression, in liberal

00:19:40.911 --> 00:19:42.721
democracies at least, is a

00:19:42.721 --> 00:19:44.221
protection against the state.

00:19:44.751 --> 00:19:46.891
It means the government cannot

00:19:46.931 --> 00:19:49.701
arrest you, silence you, or punish

00:19:49.701 --> 00:19:51.801
you for what you say or write.

00:19:52.611 --> 00:19:54.561
It does not mean freedom from

00:19:54.581 --> 00:19:55.941
other people's reactions.

00:19:56.761 --> 00:19:59.001
The Muslim communities in Bradford who

00:19:59.001 --> 00:20:00.971
burned the book, who wrote to their

00:20:01.011 --> 00:20:04.161
MPs, who organised protests — they were

00:20:04.191 --> 00:20:06.241
exercising their right of expression.

00:20:06.921 --> 00:20:08.981
The publishers who refused to

00:20:08.981 --> 00:20:11.021
stock the novel were making a

00:20:11.231 --> 00:20:13.371
commercial and moral judgment.

00:20:13.941 --> 00:20:15.821
These are all legitimate

00:20:15.821 --> 00:20:18.211
responses within a free society.

00:20:19.381 --> 00:20:22.681
The third question is perhaps the hardest

00:20:22.731 --> 00:20:25.664
to square, and it is about offence.

00:20:27.047 --> 00:20:29.407
In most liberal democracies, there

00:20:29.407 --> 00:20:31.857
are protections against “hate speech”,

00:20:32.097 --> 00:20:34.527
against “abusive or threatening speech

00:20:34.547 --> 00:20:37.027
or writing that expresses prejudice

00:20:37.257 --> 00:20:39.867
on the basis of ethnicity, religion,

00:20:40.207 --> 00:20:42.817
sexual orientation, or similar grounds.”

00:20:43.417 --> 00:20:46.757
But there is no right not to be offended.

00:20:47.367 --> 00:20:49.377
These are not the same thing.

00:20:50.427 --> 00:20:53.494
Those laws are meant to protect people.

00:20:54.144 --> 00:20:56.834
The target is speech that degrades,

00:20:57.224 --> 00:20:59.724
threatens, or incites hostility

00:20:59.754 --> 00:21:02.684
against people because of who they are.

00:21:03.594 --> 00:21:05.754
The Satanic Verses did

00:21:05.754 --> 00:21:07.014
not fit that definition.

00:21:07.924 --> 00:21:09.944
It did not say Islam was

00:21:09.944 --> 00:21:12.334
inferior, or that Muslims were

00:21:12.514 --> 00:21:14.734
dangerous or deserved ridicule.

00:21:15.694 --> 00:21:17.954
It was a literary work that played

00:21:17.974 --> 00:21:20.024
with Islamic history in ways that

00:21:20.034 --> 00:21:22.694
many Muslims found deeply offensive.

00:21:23.734 --> 00:21:25.104
That is a different thing.

00:21:26.084 --> 00:21:28.434
Religious beliefs are ideas.

00:21:28.924 --> 00:21:32.804
And in a free society, ideas can be

00:21:32.804 --> 00:21:35.844
questioned, challenged, even mocked.

00:21:36.794 --> 00:21:38.624
That is rather the point.

00:21:39.334 --> 00:21:41.654
You cannot hold a political opinion,

00:21:41.864 --> 00:21:44.284
a philosophical position, or a

00:21:44.294 --> 00:21:46.784
scientific theory above criticism

00:21:47.194 --> 00:21:48.834
simply by declaring it sacred.

00:21:50.473 --> 00:21:53.894
But religion is not, emotionally

00:21:53.894 --> 00:21:56.054
or socially, quite like choosing

00:21:56.054 --> 00:21:57.944
an opinion or a political party.

00:21:58.574 --> 00:22:01.804
For many people, their faith is not just

00:22:01.804 --> 00:22:04.244
a set of beliefs they happen to hold.

00:22:04.734 --> 00:22:07.454
It's their identity, their community,

00:22:07.734 --> 00:22:10.604
their whole understanding of the world.

00:22:11.604 --> 00:22:14.134
To attack the faith is, for

00:22:14.134 --> 00:22:16.804
them, to attack the person, the

00:22:16.804 --> 00:22:19.724
individual, and their entire world.

00:22:21.024 --> 00:22:22.924
And this brings us back

00:22:22.954 --> 00:22:24.304
to blasphemy itself.

00:22:25.474 --> 00:22:27.834
As you’ve heard, blasphemy was

00:22:27.854 --> 00:22:31.734
abolished as a crime in Britain in 2008.

00:22:32.114 --> 00:22:34.894
No idea, no matter how sacred

00:22:35.164 --> 00:22:37.194
to however many people, can be

00:22:37.194 --> 00:22:39.334
placed beyond challenge by law.

00:22:40.464 --> 00:22:43.074
But as you can see from this whole

00:22:43.074 --> 00:22:45.094
affair, in the case of Salman

00:22:45.094 --> 00:22:47.384
Rushdie, it almost didn’t matter.

00:22:48.464 --> 00:22:51.564
Yes, he was protected by UK law.

00:22:52.044 --> 00:22:54.834
But the law of one liberal democracy

00:22:55.164 --> 00:22:56.694
could not fully protect him

00:22:56.954 --> 00:22:59.024
against a transnational campaign

00:22:59.314 --> 00:23:01.384
of religiously framed intimidation.

00:23:02.304 --> 00:23:04.654
A religious leader in another country

00:23:04.904 --> 00:23:07.614
declared that he deserved to die, and

00:23:07.834 --> 00:23:09.844
the sentence has followed him around

00:23:09.844 --> 00:23:12.454
the world for more than thirty years.

00:23:13.054 --> 00:23:14.814
His translators were stabbed.

00:23:15.054 --> 00:23:16.824
His publishers were shot.

00:23:17.274 --> 00:23:20.094
His life became a series of safe houses.

00:23:21.164 --> 00:23:23.374
And when he thought he was safe,

00:23:23.824 --> 00:23:26.574
a young man he had never met tried

00:23:26.574 --> 00:23:29.494
to murder him, and almost did.

00:23:30.894 --> 00:23:33.254
So the question that remains is not

00:23:33.254 --> 00:23:35.334
whether blasphemy should be a crime.

00:23:36.134 --> 00:23:38.974
My view on that is that in a truly free

00:23:38.974 --> 00:23:41.374
society, the answer must surely be no.

00:23:42.274 --> 00:23:43.894
Everyone deserves protection

00:23:43.894 --> 00:23:45.534
from threats, discrimination, and

00:23:45.534 --> 00:23:48.524
hatred, but ideas and beliefs do

00:23:48.524 --> 00:23:50.624
not deserve immunity from criticism.

00:23:51.584 --> 00:23:53.764
The question perhaps is what we

00:23:53.814 --> 00:23:55.884
owe to people who write things

00:23:55.944 --> 00:23:57.614
that others consider blasphemous.

00:23:58.654 --> 00:24:00.724
Is "you have the right to write it"

00:24:01.234 --> 00:24:03.374
really a good enough answer when

00:24:03.374 --> 00:24:05.494
what follows is a death sentence,

00:24:05.714 --> 00:24:07.854
years in hiding, and the murder of

00:24:07.854 --> 00:24:09.904
a man who only translated the book?

00:24:11.094 --> 00:24:12.854
Rushdie himself never

00:24:12.854 --> 00:24:14.554
wavered on the principle.

00:24:15.174 --> 00:24:17.974
He has continued to argue, to this very

00:24:17.974 --> 00:24:20.924
day, that the imagination must be free.

00:24:21.424 --> 00:24:24.084
That to accept any other position

00:24:24.314 --> 00:24:27.524
was to hand a veto to whoever was

00:24:27.534 --> 00:24:29.564
willing to threaten the most violence.

00:24:30.484 --> 00:24:33.364
And for that principle, he paid a

00:24:33.364 --> 00:24:35.564
price that most people who hold that a

00:24:35.564 --> 00:24:38.314
similar view will never be asked to pay.

00:24:40.494 --> 00:24:42.684
OK then, that is it for this

00:24:42.694 --> 00:24:44.124
three-part mini-series on

00:24:44.124 --> 00:24:45.904
blasphemy and free expression.

00:24:46.404 --> 00:24:47.874
We started with a student

00:24:47.874 --> 00:24:49.946
in Edinburgh in 1697.

00:24:50.124 --> 00:24:51.314
We ended with a novelist

00:24:51.324 --> 00:24:53.287
in New York in 2022.

00:24:53.886 --> 00:24:55.954
In between, three centuries of

00:24:55.984 --> 00:24:58.114
argument about who has the right to

00:24:58.114 --> 00:25:00.864
say what, who gets to judge, and what

00:25:00.864 --> 00:25:02.994
happens to those who test the limits.

00:25:03.444 --> 00:25:04.424
I hope you found it as

00:25:04.444 --> 00:25:06.224
interesting to listen to as I

00:25:06.224 --> 00:25:08.024
found it to research and write.

00:25:08.544 --> 00:25:10.274
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:10.274 --> 00:25:12.774
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:13.184 --> 00:25:15.894
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:15.894 --> 00:25:18.104
I'll catch you in the next episode.

